Given the partial differential equation \[\frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}\] This can be rewritten as \[\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} - \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = 0\] <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 1 (Discriminant Test)</div>To classify a second-order linear PDE, we compare it with the general form \[A\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2} + B\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x\partial t} + C\frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial t^2} + D\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} + E\frac{\partial f}{\partial t} + Ff = G\] For the given equation, \(A = 1,\ B = 0,\ C = 0.\) The classification depends on the discriminant \[\Delta = B^2 - 4AC\] \[\Delta = 0^2-4(1)(0) = 0\] For a second-order PDE, \[\begin{cases}
    \Delta &gt;0 &amp; \text{Hyperbolic}\\ \Delta =0 &amp; \text{Parabolic}\\ \Delta &lt;0 &amp; \text{Elliptic}
\end{cases}\] Since \(\Delta = 0,\) the PDE is <strong>parabolic</strong>.</div> <div class="sol-block sol-method"><div class="sol-block-title">Method 2 (Recognizing Standard PDE Forms)</div>The equation \[\frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x^2}\] is the one-dimensional heat (diffusion) equation. Since the given PDE is the heat equation, it is <strong>parabolic.</strong></div> \[\boxed{\text{(A) Parabolic}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div>Standard classifications of common PDEs are: \[\text{Heat Equation}\;\Rightarrow\;\text{Parabolic}\] \[\text{Wave Equation}\;\Rightarrow\;\text{Hyperbolic}\] \[\text{Laplace Equation}\;\Rightarrow\;\text{Elliptic}\]</div>

Given \[f(x)=e^{-x}, \qquad 0&lt;x&lt;\infty\] The required probability is \[P(X&gt;1)=\int_{1}^{\infty} e^{-x}\,dx\] \[P(X&gt;1)=\left[-e^{-x}\right]_{1}^{\infty}
=0+\frac{1}{e}
=e^{-1}\] \[P(X&gt;1)\approx 0.3679\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }0.368}\]

Given delta to star conversion is shown below. <img src="img/8762d565d1f06037.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Before scaling, \[R_A=\frac{R_bR_c}{R_a+R_b+R_c}\] If \[R_a'=kR_a,\qquad R_b'=kR_b,\qquad R_c'=kR_c\] then after scaling, \[R_A'=\frac{R_b'R_c'}{R_a'+R_b'+R_c'}
=\frac{(kR_b)(kR_c)}{kR_a+kR_b+kR_c}\] \[R_A'=\frac{k^2R_bR_c}{k(R_a+R_b+R_c)}
=kR_A\] Hence, each star resistance scales by a factor of \(k\). \[\boxed{\text{B) k}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>If all elements of a \(\Delta\) network are multiplied by a factor \(k\), all corresponding \(Y\) elements are also multiplied by \(k\).</li></ul></div>

Given: \[C_1=10\,\mu\text{F},\quad V_1=10\,\text{V}\] \[C_2=5\,\mu\text{F},\quad V_2=5\,\text{V}\] \[C_3=2\,\mu\text{F},\quad V_3=2\,\text{V}\] <img src="img/4102f5ed10b26da1.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Since \(C_3\) has the minimum breakdown voltage, it determines the maximum safe voltage across the network. <img src="img/ad77a68f2729d5b9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Using voltage division for series capacitors, \[V_3=\frac{C_2}{C_2+C_3}V_s\] \[2=\frac{5}{5+2}V_s\] \[V_s=2.8\,\text{V}\] The equivalent capacitance is \[C_{\text{eq}}=C_1+\frac{C_2C_3}{C_2+C_3}
=10+\frac{5\times2}{5+2}
=\frac{80}{7}\,\mu\text{F}\] Hence, \[Q=C_{\text{eq}}V_s
=\frac{80}{7}\times2.8
=32\,\mu\text{C}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) 2.8 and 32}}\]

According to the question, the circuit is shown below, <img src="img/098fe438af2e98b3.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Internal impedance/source impedance is given as, \[Z_s=R_s+jX_s=(4+j3)\ \Omega\] For a purely resistive load connected to a complex source impedance, maximum power is transferred when \[R_L=|Z_s|
=\sqrt{R_s^2+X_s^2}\] \[R_L=\sqrt{4^2+3^2}
=5\ \Omega\] (C) \(5~\Omega\)

Given circuit is shown below, <img src="img/c41596a1528c5532.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,448px)"> Thevenin's equivalent voltage across load \(R_L\) is \(V_{TH}\). \[V_{TH}=V_{OC}\] For open circuit condition, \[I_2=0\] Hence, the load resistance \(R_L\) is removed. Since, \[j40I_2=0\] the dependent voltage source becomes zero. Thus, the circuit becomes, <img src="img/3ef67a6edbd71bbe.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,384px)"> From the circuit, \[V_{TH}=V_{OC}=10V_L\] Applying KVL in the left loop, \[V_s-3I_1-j4I_1=0\] \[I_1=\frac{V_s}{3+j4}
=\frac{100\angle53.13^\circ}{5\angle53.13^\circ}\] \[I_1=20\angle0^\circ~\text{A}\] Therefore, \[V_L=j4I_1
=j4\times20\angle0^\circ\] \[V_L=80\angle90^\circ~\text{V}\] Hence, \[V_{TH}=V_{OC}=10V_L\] \[V_{TH}=800\angle90^\circ~\text{V}\] (C) \(800\angle90^\circ\)

For the first op-amp, the non-inverting terminal is connected to \(+1\,\text{V}.\) <img src="img/e8f69a717ae0349d.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Hence, by the virtual short concept, \[V_{-}=V_{+}=1\,\text{V}.\] Applying KCL at the inverting terminal, \[\frac{1-(-2)}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
+\frac{1-V_{1}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}=0,\] where \(V_{1}\) is the output of the first op-amp. \[3+1-V_{1}=0\] \[V_{1}=4\,\text{V}.\] The second op-amp is a non-inverting amplifier with \[R_{f}=1\,\text{k}\Omega,\qquad
R_{g}=1\,\text{k}\Omega.\] Hence, its gain is \[A_{v}=1+\frac{R_{f}}{R_{g}}
=1+\frac{1}{1}=2.\] Therefore, \[V_{\text{out}}
=2\times 4
=8\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }8\,\text{V}}\]

The circuit is an inverting amplifier. Its closed-loop gain is \[A_{v}=-\frac{R_{f}}{R_{i}}
=-\frac{470\,\text{k}\Omega}{22\,\text{k}\Omega}
=-21.36.\] The input signal is \[V_{in}=0.25\sin(\omega t)\,\text{V}.\] Hence, the output amplitude is \[V_{o(\text{max})}=21.36\times0.25
=5.34\,\text{V}.\] For a sinusoidal output, \[\left(\frac{dV_{o}}{dt}\right)_{\max}
=2\pi fV_{o(\text{max})}.\] To avoid slew-rate distortion, \[2\pi fV_{o(\text{max})}\leq \text{SR}.\] Given, \[\text{SR}=0.8\,\text{V}/\mu\text{s}
=0.8\times10^{6}\,\text{V/s}.\] Therefore, \[f_{\max}
=\frac{0.8\times10^{6}}
{2\pi(5.34)}
\approx23.84\times10^{3}\,\text{Hz}.\] \[f_{\max}\approx23.84\,\text{kHz}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }23.84\,\text{kHz}}\]

The circuit consists of two cascaded active high-pass filters. For the first stage, \[f_{c1}=\frac{1}{2\pi RC}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(1\,\text{k}\Omega)(0.1\,\mu\text{F})}
\approx1591.5\,\text{Hz}.\] For the second stage, \[f_{c2}=\frac{1}{2\pi RC}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(2\,\text{k}\Omega)(1\,\mu\text{F})}
\approx79.6\,\text{Hz}.\] Hence, the overall filter passes frequencies greater than both cutoff frequencies, i.e., \[f&gt;1591.5\,\text{Hz}.\] The input signal is \[V_{i}(t)=10+10\sin100\pi t+10\sin4000\pi t+10\sin100000\pi t.\] The corresponding frequency components are \[\begin{aligned}
10 &amp;\rightarrow 0\,\text{Hz},\\
10\sin100\pi t &amp;\rightarrow 50\,\text{Hz},\\
10\sin4000\pi t &amp;\rightarrow 2000\,\text{Hz},\\
10\sin100000\pi t &amp;\rightarrow 50000\,\text{Hz}.
\end{aligned}\] Since both \(0\,\text{Hz}\) and \(50\,\text{Hz}\) are heavily attenuated, while \(2000\,\text{Hz}\) and \(50000\,\text{Hz}\) pass through, the least attenuated frequency component is \[\boxed{\text{(c) }2\,\text{KHz}}\]

Given \[G(s)=\frac{1}{(s+1)^3}\] \[G(j\omega)=\frac{1}{(1+j\omega)^3}\] The phase is, \[\angle G(j\omega)
=
-3\tan^{-1}\omega\] At the ultimate frequency, \(\angle G(j\omega_u)=-180^\circ\) \[-3\tan^{-1}\omega_u=-180^\circ\] \[\omega_u=\sqrt{3}\;\text{rad/s}\] Hence, \(T_u=\dfrac{2\pi}{\omega_u}
=\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt3}\) The magnitude at \(\omega_u\) is, \[|G(j\omega_u)|
=
\dfrac{1}{(1+\omega_u^2)^{3/2}}
=
\dfrac{1}{(1+3)^{3/2}}
=
\dfrac18\] Therefore, \[K_u=\frac1{|G(j\omega_u)|}=8\] \[\boxed{(C)\;8\text{ and }\dfrac{2\pi}{\sqrt3}}\]

From Q10.13.1, \(K_u=8.\) According to the Ziegler-Nichols tuning rule, \(K=0.5K_u=4.\) The transfer function from the disturbance input to the output is, \[\frac{Y(s)}{D(s)}
=
\frac{1}{1+KG(s)}\] At \(\omega=\dfrac{2\pi}{T_u}=\sqrt3,\) \[KG(j\omega)
=
4\left(\frac18\angle-180^\circ\right)
=
-\frac12\] \[\therefore\;
\dfrac{Y(j\omega)}{D(j\omega)}
=
\dfrac1{1-\dfrac12}
=
2\] \[\boxed{(D)\;2.0}\]

This is a linear programming problem. We find the maximum value of the objective function \(f(X,Y) = 3X + 6Y\) subject to the constraints: \[2X + Y \le 6\] \[X + 2Y \le 8\] Since \(X, Y\) are positive real numbers, we look at the intersection point of the boundary lines: \[\begin{aligned}
2X + Y &amp;= 6 \quad \text{--- (1)} \\
X + 2Y &amp;= 8 \quad \text{--- (2)}
\end{aligned}\] Multiply equation (1) by 2: \[4X + 2Y = 12\] Subtract equation (2) from this result: \[3X = 4 \implies X = \dfrac{4}{3}\] Substitute \(X = \dfrac{4}{3}\) into equation (1): \[2\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right) + Y = 6 \implies Y = 6 - \dfrac{8}{3} = \dfrac{10}{3}\] The intersection corner point is \(\left(\dfrac{4}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\). Let us check the objective function value at this vertex: <ul><li>At \(\left(\dfrac{4}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\): \(f(X,Y) = 3\left(\dfrac{4}{3}\right) + 6\left(\dfrac{10}{3}\right) = 4 + 20 = 24\).</li></ul> \[\boxed{\text{A:  (4/3, 10/3)}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Corner Point Theorem</strong>: In linear programming, the maximum or minimum value of an objective function always occurs at one of the corner vertices of the feasible region.</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not choose points like \(\left(\dfrac{8}{3}, \dfrac{10}{3}\right)\) without verifying if they violate the problem constraints (\(2(8/3) + 10/3 = 26/3 &gt; 6\)).</li></ul></div>

To solve the summation, we rationalize the denominator of the general term: \[T_n = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n} + \sqrt{n+1}}\] Multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate \((\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1})\): \[T_n = \frac{\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1}}{n - (n+1)} = \frac{\sqrt{n} - \sqrt{n+1}}{-1} = \sqrt{n+1} - \sqrt{n}\] Expanding the series from \(n=1\) to \(n=80\) creates a telescoping sum: \[\begin{aligned}
S &amp;= (\sqrt{2} - \sqrt{1}) + (\sqrt{3} - \sqrt{2}) + (\sqrt{4} - \sqrt{3}) + \dots + (\sqrt{81} - \sqrt{80})
\end{aligned}\] All intermediate terms cancel out perfectly, leaving only the first negative and last positive terms: \[S = \sqrt{81} - \sqrt{1} = 9 - 1 = 8\] \[\boxed{\text{B: 8}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Telescoping Series</strong>: Rationalizing fractional terms containing radical differences often reveals a pattern where internal adjacent terms eliminate each other.</li></ul></div>
